The following C# code uses Roslyn, which is a .NET Compiler Platform (powered by the .NET compiler provided as part of .NET Core 3.0 and later), to accomplish this task. It will extract the comments, then generate the appropriate Assert statement.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Syntax;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var code = File.ReadAllText(@"Path_to_code");
        var syntaxTree = CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(code,
            CSharpParseOptions.Default.WithLanguageVersion(LanguageVersion.CSharp7_3));

        var root = (CompilationUnitSyntax)syntaxTree.GetRoot();

        var semanticModel = SyntaxTree.GetSemanticModel(syntaxTree);

        foreach (var member in root.Members.FlattenBreadthFirst())
        {
            if (member is SyntaxNode syntaxNode && syntaxNode.Kind() == SyntaxKind.SingleLineDocumentationCommentTrivia)
            {
                var text = syntaxNode.ToString().Trim();
                if(text.StartsWith("@@@")) 
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"// {text}");
                    var type = semanticModel.GetTypeInfo(syntaxNode.Parent).Type;
                    Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert({type.Name} = ...);");
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Please note that this code will only output Assert statements for the comments that are placed directly above statements. If a comment is placed above a block of code, the corresponding Assert statement has to be derived from that block of code. In that sense, writing an analyzer that does this is more complex.

The Roslyn tool provides a powerful API for parsing and analyzing C# code, which requires quite a bit of setup but offers a rich semantic model that allows such a complex task. Please ensure to have a .NET SDK and .NET Compiler Platform installed and configured properly.--------------------The following is a Python solution for reading the C# source code and outputting a text file with the assert statements for each "@@@" comment found in the code:

```python
import re

# path to the C# source file
FILE_PATH = "your_file_here"

def read_csharp_file(file_path):
    # pattern for "@@@" comments
    PATTERN = re.compile(r'"@@@\s*(.*)')
    
    with open(file_path, 'r') as f:
        lines = f.readlines()

    # find all "@@@" comments
    comments = []
    for line in lines:
        match = PATTERN.match(line)
        if match:
            comments.append(match.group(1))
    
    return comments

def write_assertions(file_path, asserts):
    # write to a new file
    with open('assertions.txt', 'w') as f:
        for assert_line in asserts:
            f.write(f"<code>\nDebug.Assert({assert_line});\n</code>\n")


assertions = read_csharp_file(FILE_PATH)
write_assertions('assertions.txt', assertions)
```

This solution reads the file line by line, checks for "@@@" comments, and saves them. It will then output these assertions in a new file named `assertions.txt` in a format that can be conveniently copy-pasted into existing C# code. Note that you need to modify `FILE_PATH` with the path to your actual C# file.--------------------I can't write full code, but I can write a Python solution for the problem which provides an idea on how to solve it. I am using Python's re module which provides support for regular expressions.

